.TH STAT 2
.SH NAME
stat, fstat, wstat, dirread \- get and put file status, read directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "stat :: proc(path: string, out: ^abi.Stat) -> i64"
.B "fstat :: proc(fd: int, out: ^abi.Stat) -> i64"
.B "wstat :: proc(path: string, s: ^abi.Stat) -> i64"
.B "dirread :: proc(fd: int, entries: []abi.Dirent) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Stat
resolves
.I path
in the caller's name space and writes what the file's server says about
it into
.IR out .
.I Fstat
does the same for an already open descriptor; its
.B Stat
carries an empty name, because the kernel keeps no name for an open file.
Both answer zero on success.
.PP
An
.B abi.Stat
is:
.IP
.EX
Stat :: struct {
	qid_path:    u64,
	qid_version: u32,
	qid_kind:    u8, // QTDIR and friends, as 9P spells them
	name_len:    u8,
	mode:        u32,
	length:      u64,
	atime:       u64,
	mtime:       u64,
	name:        [NAME_MAX]u8,
}
.EE
.PP
.B Qid_path
and
.B qid_version
together identify the file: two files with the same
.B qid_path
and
.B qid_version
are the same file, and
.B qid_version
changes each time its content changes.
.B Qid_kind
carries
.BR abi.QTDIR ,
set when the file is a directory.
.B Mode
carries the permission bits, the low nine of Plan 9's
.BR Dir.mode ,
with
.B abi.DMDIR
set at the top for a directory.
.B Length
is the file's size in bytes, zero for a directory.
.B Atime
and
.B mtime
are seconds since the epoch.
.B Name
holds the last element of the path that was asked about, of length
.BR name_len ,
up to
.B abi.NAME_MAX
(64) bytes.
.PP
.I Wstat
writes
.IR s 's
.B mode
and
.B length
back to the file at
.IR path ,
which is what a program may change about a file today; the record's
other fields are read and ignored, unlike Plan 9's convention of
\(lqdon't care\(rq sentinel values.
.PP
.I Dirread
fills
.I entries
from the open directory
.IR fd ,
as many as fit or as the directory holds, and answers how many it filled.
Zero is the end of the directory.
The descriptor's offset advances by what
.I dirread
consumed, so a second call continues where the first left off; an entry
that did not fit is read again on the next call rather than lost.
An
.B abi.Dirent
carries the same qid and name fields as a
.BR Stat ,
and nothing else: anything more about one entry is a further
.I stat
away.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B sys/abi/abi.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR open (2),
.IR seek (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each call answers zero, or a count for
.IR dirread ,
on success, and a negative
.B \-errno
on failure; see
.IR errstr (2).
.B ENOTDIR
is
.I dirread
on a descriptor that is not a directory.
